    "design_decisions": "AisleConfig uses canonical + aliases on AisleItem with declaration-order first-match lookup. MealPlan serializer is canonical (no source-format preservation); YAML block lists are joined as comma-separated scalars. ShoppingList uses source-range replacement so only the bracket marker character is mutated. parseSectionHeader is checked before parseItem, which is intentional for the meal-plan format.",
    "test_coverage_notes": "All three parsers have dedicated test suites. AisleConfigParser: 9 tests covering empty input, comments, fixture section order, canonical/alias lookup, multi-word tokens, whitespace trimming, case-insensitivity, same-token across sections, and orphan items. MealPlanParser: 7 tests covering empty input, frontmatter, day blocks with ordinal, unnamed sections, recipe/ingredient discrimination, line comments, and fixture round-trip. ShoppingList: 9 tests covering empty documents, fixture item count/content, checked state detection, toggle, toggle no-op, append (empty and non-empty), remove, remove no-op, and byte-fidelity of mutations. Coverage is adequate for the assigned scope.",
    "known_limitations": "1. AisleConfigParser stores empty alias strings from trailing pipe separators. 2. ShoppingList does not handle CRLF line endings. 3. MealPlanParser.serialise does not append a trailing newline. 4. parseSectionHeader has priority over parseItem so a contrived @foo: line would be misclassified. 5. MealPlan frontmatter serializer does not preserve YAML block list structure (joins with comma)."
  },
